\section*{Introduction}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Introduction}
\textbf{The disease.} Mosaic variegated aneuploidy is a rare recessive disorder of chromosome segregation. {% if domain_map %}{{ domain_map.protein | tex }}, the product of {{ domain_map.gene | tex }}, {% else %}BubR1 {% endif %}is a core component of the mitotic checkpoint complex, which holds the anaphase-promoting complex inactive until every chromosome is correctly attached to the spindle. That checkpoint stops a cell dividing before its chromosomes are ready. When {% if domain_map %}{{ domain_map.protein | tex }}{% else %}BubR1{% endif %} is reduced the brake releases early, sister chromatids separate prematurely, and each division can leave a daughter cell with a different wrong chromosome count. The result is not one aneuploidy but many, varying between cells, and the syndrome is diagnosed on that pattern {{ cite('[@hanks2004]') | tex }}. Because it is recessive, one working allele is enough; disease requires that both copies be compromised, and severity tracks how little functional protein remains {{ cite('[@suijkerbuijk2010]') | tex }}.

\textbf{The variants found in this child.}{% if domain_map and truncating %}{% for v in truncating %} {{ v.hgvsp | tex }}, a {{ v.consequence | replace('_', ' ') | tex }} variant, truncates {{ domain_map.protein | tex }} at residue {{ v.position }} of {{ domain_map.length }}, before the kinase domain begins.{% endfor %}{% for w in nontruncating %} {{ w.hgvsp | tex }} is a missense change inside that same kinase domain.{% endfor %} Both were found in this analysis rather than supplied with the challenge data; the Methods give the filter that returned them, and Section~\ref{sec:mechanism} places each on the protein.{% else %} Not available.{% endif %}

\textbf{The consequence.} Two alleles compromised in the same protein leave less working {% if domain_map %}{{ domain_map.protein | tex }}{% else %}BubR1{% endif %} than a dividing cell needs. Constitutional chromosomal instability follows, which disturbs growth and development and carries a cancer predisposition, because a cell population that keeps generating new karyotypes keeps sampling oncogenic ones; childhood rhabdomyosarcoma, Wilms tumour and leukaemia are the tumours reported in this syndrome {{ cite('[@pavone2022]') | tex }}. How much working protein remains is therefore the quantity any proposed medicine has to act on. Section~\ref{sec:problem} sets out why the protein itself cannot be drugged, and Section~\ref{sec:stage1b} proposes what can be done instead.

