\subsubsection{What a readthrough agent would produce at this stop codon}\label{sec:readthrough}
A nonsense allele is a readthrough target only if three things hold. Each is computed from this run's own data, and one of the three comes out against the strategy.

\textbf{Which stop codon.} The codon change is \texttt{ {{- rt.codon_change | tex -}} }, so the premature stop is \textbf{ {{- rt.stop_codon | tex -}} }, the {{ rt.stop_permissiveness | tex }} of the three stop codons and the one ataluren was characterised against {{ cite('[@ng2021]') | tex }}. The reference codon was re-read from the genome at \texttt{ {{- rt.codon_region | tex -}} } and {{ 'matches the transcript annotation' if rt.reference_codon_confirmed else 'does NOT match the transcript annotation, so the coordinates below are unreliable' }}.

\textbf{What 3' context.} The base immediately after the stop exerts the strongest single-nucleotide influence on readthrough efficiency. Here it is \textbf{ {{- rt.plus4_base_rna | tex -}} }, giving the context {{ rt.stop_context | tex }}, which is position {{ rt.plus4_rank }} of {{ rt.plus4_of }} in the published order {{ rt.plus4_order | tex }} {{ cite('[@mangkalaphiban2024]') | tex }}. The codon favours readthrough and the local context works against it. Both are reported, so the weakness of the strategy stands beside its strength.

\textbf{What protein.} Readthrough does not restore the reference residue. Near-cognate decoding at {{ rt.stop_codon | tex }} inserts Trp, Arg or Cys {{ cite('[@ng2021]') | tex }}, so a readthrough agent would produce one of three specific full-length proteins rather than the wild-type one. AlphaMissense scores each of them, and Figure~\ref{fig:readthrough_products} places them among every other substitution the residue could carry:

