Verdict: \textbf{ {{- rt.verdict | tex -}} }. The residue is tolerant more broadly; of the {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_substitutions_scored }} substitutions AlphaMissense scores at this position, {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_benign }} are benign, {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_ambiguous }} ambiguous and {{ rt.residue_tolerance.n_pathogenic }} pathogenic, with a mean of {{ rt.residue_tolerance.mean_score }} against {{ rt.regional_means['whole protein'] }} for the protein as a whole. The position sits in the {{ rt.residue_region | tex }}, not inside a folded domain. So the predicted readthrough product is a full-length protein carrying a tolerated substitution at a tolerant position between two domains.

\textbf{Why the combination, not readthrough alone.} The premature stop lies {{ rt.nmd_distance_to_last_junction_nt }} nt upstream of the last exon--exon junction, in exon {{ rt.ptc_exon | tex }}, and is therefore {{ rt.nmd_verdict | tex }}. That is the reason to pair a readthrough agent with nonsense-mediated-decay inhibition, not an argument against the axis: most of the transcript is degraded before it is translated, so a readthrough agent acts on a depleted substrate. The pharmacology is the weak point, though not in the way a flat ``no NMD inhibitor exists'' would suggest. No drug has been developed or approved \emph{for} NMD inhibition, so no agent carries it as an intended, dose-characterised mechanism. A systematic profiling of FDA-approved drugs for NMD modulation found most have unremarkable effects {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') | tex }}; the ones that do inhibit it do so off-target. They are amlexanox, the agent carried on this axis, where the literature is thin {{ cite('[@atanasova2017]') | tex }}{{ cite('[@benslimane2023]') | tex }}; the cardiac glycosides, from a cell-based screen, acting through Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition and raised intracellular calcium {{ cite('[@nickless2014]') | tex }}; 5-azacytidine, at concentrations reported to match levels reached in patients {{ cite('[@bhuvanagiri2014]') | tex }}; and homoharringtonine, the single consistent hit in that FDA-wide screen {{ cite('[@zhao2022]') | tex }}. The pairing itself is not hypothetical: an NMD inhibitor combined with a readthrough agent raised protein from the mutant transcript in patient-derived cells for another nonsense disorder {{ cite('[@amarschwartz2023]') | tex }}. What bounds it is this child. 5-azacytidine and homoharringtonine are myelosuppressive anticancer drugs, and adding either to a regimen this report already flags for therapy-related myeloid neoplasia risk in a chromosomal instability syndrome is the wrong direction; the cardiac glycosides are renally cleared with a narrow therapeutic index against a background of nephrocalcinosis, alongside an anthracycline. Underneath all of them sits a mechanism-level concern: NMD is a surveillance pathway with tumour-suppressive roles, and every one of those agents suppresses it globally, which in a cancer-predisposition syndrome is a risk that no choice among them removes. None was carried as a candidate, and Section~\ref{sec:limitations} says so.

\textbf{The modality the mechanism points to, outside the approved list.} That specificity problem has a published solution. Gene-specific antisense inhibition of NMD uses oligonucleotides that block exon-junction-complex deposition downstream of a premature stop, attenuating decay of one transcript while leaving NMD intact everywhere else {{ cite('[@nomakuchi2016]') | tex }}, and it was combined with a readthrough compound in that first report to restore full-length protein from a nonsense allele --- the exact pairing this axis needs. It has since been developed toward the clinic for the CFTR W1282X allele, where a cocktail of three such oligonucleotides raised mutant CFTR mRNA, protein and chloride current in human bronchial epithelial cells {{ cite('[@kim2022]') | tex }}.

The design inputs for this child are from the analysis above: the premature stop sits in exon {{ rt.ptc_exon | tex if rt else '' }} with {{ nmd.ptcs[0].exons_downstream_of_ptc if nmd and nmd.ptcs else '' }} exons downstream of it, and those downstream junctions are the candidate target sites. What this approach does not solve is delivery. Oligonucleotide biodistribution is tissue-restricted, the cystic fibrosis work targeted an epithelium that inhaled delivery reaches, and which tissue to target in a disorder of every dividing cell is unresolved. It is not an approved medicine and is therefore not a candidate here; it is recorded because it is the one strategy whose stated rationale matches this report's own objection to the systemic agents.

Whether the small percentage of full-length protein that readthrough typically restores would cross the phenotypic threshold is unknown, and the conditional framing in the residual-function paragraph above applies unchanged {{ cite('[@simmons2019]') | tex }}.
