{% if consanguinity_excluded %}
\textbf{Recurrent parental pregnancy loss is consistent with biallelic lethality.} Recurrent spontaneous abortion (HP:0200067) is among the released features. The argument rests on a premise this data cannot verify: that each parent carries one of the two variants. No parental genotypes were released, and either variant could be de novo, in which case the computation below does not apply and the consistency that follows is coincidence. Granting inheritance: runs of homozygosity give $F_{ROH}$($\geq$1.5 Mb) = {{ roh_f }}, so the parents are not detectably related, and each pregnancy of two unrelated carriers has a 25\% chance of being biallelic. Complete knockout in mouse is embryonic-lethal with complete penetrance, between implantation and placentation. If biallelic loss is lethal in humans too, recurrent early pregnancy loss is the predicted consequence, and it is what the family history records. Under the carrier-parents premise, that is human evidence, from this child's own family, for the premise the whole null-plus-hypomorph argument rests on. It is consistent with those premises rather than proof of either: recurrent miscarriage is common and has many causes, no products of conception were genotyped, and the parents were not genotyped.
{% endif %}

\textbf{Residual function: a conditional inference.} {{ cite('[@+simmons2019]') }} report that nearly complete loss of BubR1 is required for microcephaly. Microcephaly is not among the features released to participants. \textbf{If} it is genuinely absent rather than unreported or unassessed, that would imply the missense allele retains meaningful activity, and strategies acting on residual protein would be acting on a partially functional system. The antecedent is not established here and is not asserted, under the feature-absence rule. Two further cautions apply --- microcephaly is reported in 87\% of MVA1 cases but is explicitly not required for the diagnosis {{ cite('[@callier2005]') }}, and this child is young, so a head-circumference trajectory may not be complete. The inference is recorded because it would matter if resolved, not because it is resolved.

