This disorder is a loss of mitotic fidelity. Almost every drug that exists near this pathway would deepen the defect rather than compensate for it.

That asymmetry is the central problem of Track 2 for this gene, and it is why the pipeline encodes it as a rule rather than as a warning: \emph{an inhibitor of a mitotic-fidelity gene is contraindicated in a mitotic-fidelity disorder.} Candidates are tested against it automatically, and the ones it rejects are listed below rather than dropped silently.

\textbf{So the strategy is compensation, not replacement.} No available molecule restores BubR1 kinase activity. What a repurposing hypothesis can do is act on something \emph{other than} the broken protein so that the consequences of its loss are reduced. Each candidate axis is one such mechanism, and each is a hypothesis about a different point of leverage:

\begin{itemize}
\item restore some full-length protein from the nonsense allele (readthrough, NMD inhibition) --- \textbf{populated as the readthrough axis below}, and characterised in Section~\ref{sec:readthrough};
\item stabilise the partially functional protein the missense allele already makes;
\item reduce the cellular cost of the aneuploidy that results (proteostasis and chaperone support) --- \textbf{populated as the proteostasis axis below};
\item treat downstream consequences that are themselves druggable, where the spindle checkpoint is not.
\end{itemize}

The candidate list below is organised by that logic, and by how well each axis is supported for this child rather than for the syndrome in general.

