\subsubsection*{What the mechanism implies for standard-regimen therapy}
This is a mechanism finding, not a repurposing candidate, and it bears on therapy given under standard rhabdomyosarcoma protocols --- which protocol this child is on, and whether treatment is ongoing, the released record does not say. It is a consideration for the treating team. It is \textbf{not} a recommendation to alter a rhabdomyosarcoma protocol, and nothing here licenses withdrawing therapy given with curative intent.

\textbf{The vincristine asymmetry.} Vincristine kills by disrupting the spindle so that the spindle assembly checkpoint holds the cell in mitosis until it dies there. A cell with a weakened checkpoint does not arrest; it slips through mitosis and survives. That is a documented resistance mechanism for antimitotic drugs: checkpoint-compromised cells are less sensitive to paclitaxel {{ cite('[@sudo2004]') | tex }} and to microtubule-targeted drugs generally {{ cite('[@lee2004]') | tex }}, mitotic slippage is the established route to that resistance {{ cite('[@cheng2017]') | tex }}, and the one report specific to this protein runs the same way in reverse: BubR1 is repressed in acute myeloid leukaemia, and restoring it re-sensitises the cells to antimitotic therapy {{ cite('[@schnerch2013]') | tex }}.

This child's checkpoint defect is germline, so it is present in the tumour as well as in normal tissue. Two consequences follow, both stated as hypotheses:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Efficacy.} The tumour may be intrinsically less sensitive to vincristine's cytotoxic mechanism than a checkpoint-competent tumour of the same histology.
\item \textbf{Toxicity.} Vincristine's dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral neuropathy, which acts through axonal microtubule transport in post-mitotic neurons. That mechanism does not involve the checkpoint, so it is not reduced by the defect. This child is additionally a CYP3A5 non-expresser, the higher-neuropathy-risk genotype in the reports cited in the pharmacogenomic section.
\end{enumerate}
For the same drug in the same child, plausibly reduced efficacy and plausibly undiminished or increased toxicity. That asymmetry is the finding.

\textbf{The DNA-damaging agents run the same way by a different route.} Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, doxorubicin, etoposide and radiotherapy do not act through the checkpoint, so the slippage argument does not apply to them. The argument for caution there is the one already made in this report: a chromosomal-instability background plus mutagenic exposure raises the risk of a therapy-related second malignancy. The single published rhabdomyosarcoma in this syndrome was treated with reduced-intensity chemotherapy {{ cite('[@nishitaniisa2019]') | tex }}. That paper's full text was not accessible to this analysis, so it is cited for the approach its title states and not for any regimen detail, which would have to be read from the paper before being built on.

\textbf{Limits of the analogy, and of the inference.} De-intensification protocols in ataxia telangiectasia, Nijmegen breakage syndrome and Fanconi anaemia rest on DNA-repair defects ({{ cite('[@-sandoval1998]') | tex }}; {{ cite('[@-bienemann2011]') | tex }}; {{ cite('[@-gluckman1983]') | tex }}); this is a segregation defect, so the analogy is suggestive rather than direct. The antimitotic-resistance literature comes from cancer cell lines with acquired checkpoint loss, not from germline hypomorphs. The tumour's own checkpoint status is unknown, because no tumour sequencing was released, and a somatic event could have moved it in either direction. The anti-target rule governs new proposals only and is not applied to therapy given with curative intent.

\textbf{A related risk the same mechanism raises.} Alkylating agents and topoisomerase II poisons, both components of standard therapy, are the established causes of therapy-related myeloid neoplasia, and this is a chromosomal instability and cancer-predisposition syndrome. The combination supports an elevated plausible risk of therapy-related secondary malignancy, bearing on cumulative-dose decisions and long-term haematological surveillance rather than on any protocol change; radiosensitivity, by contrast, is unresolved for this disorder and is recorded as an open question, not a finding. The full argument, with the pharmacogenomic findings for this context and the evidence behind both, is in Section~\ref{sec:pgxcontexts}.

