\subsection{Pharmacogenomic safety and feasibility context}\label{sec:pgx}
\textbf{This section is not a prescribing recommendation, and it is not the deliverable.} The deliverable is the repurposing hypotheses above. Pharmacogenomics earns its place here for three specific reasons:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{It gates candidates.} A repurposing hypothesis is only viable if the child can take the drug. MT-RNR1 decides whether the aminoglycoside readthrough candidates are usable at all, so that genotype is a precondition for a candidate rather than a safety aside: it is reported with those candidates in Section~\ref{sec:stage1b}, where the verdict table shows the join.
\item \textbf{It establishes the safety context a candidate would enter.} Any proposed addition has to sit alongside standard cytotoxic therapy, the interaction between a chromosomal instability syndrome and that therapy is stated with the regimen in Section~\ref{sec:stage1b}.
\item \textbf{It demonstrates that the calling method changes answers.} The copy-number counterfactual below shows a phenotype call flipping on whether the pipeline detects a gene duplication --- a claim about method rigour, not about this child's prescriptions.
\end{enumerate}

